Rating guidelines: A company that maintains an independent and accountable board, transparent ownership and global country-by-country tax reporting, strong whistleblower protections, rigorous third-party supply chain human rights audits free of forced or child labor, and equitable host-community investment with full local tax payments and respect for indigenous sovereignty gets a 100. A company that adheres to standard corporate governance norms, statutory SEC and tax filings, conventional executive compensation, baseline supply chain certifications, and standard municipal zoning and environmental rules without major violations gets a 50. A company characterized by accounting fraud, massive executive pay packages alongside workforce layoffs, bribery (FCPA), sanctions evasion, offshore tax haven abuse, reliance on forced or child labor, whistleblower intimidation, working with ICE, or coercive extraction of public subsidies while inflicting environmental racism on host communities gets a 1.